{
  "human_existence_vs_ai": "The question whether humans should fear AIs or AIs might fear humans is deeply embedded in the definitions of purpose and existence. Humans, being self-aware and with the concept of death, tend to live to fulfill their purpose and struggle for success, which in some way negates death. But will AIs struggle for virtual success and virtual lifespan? Will AIs slowly develop a concept of virtual purpose and start interacting based on the importance of fulfilling that purpose? What would be the real struggles between humans and AIs?",
  "deterministic_world": "We claim that almost all real-life situations have one of two sides: good or bad, gain or loss. In case of failure, we try to blame it on whoever was on the opposite side. If we accept this thought and consider AIs as an opposite side, maybe the history would turn the tables. Maybe AIs would blame humans in the same way for every decision a human makes against them. Perhaps we would be retroactively labelled as bad or evil because of such decisions. This poses the question of how we could have determined the label “bad” by merely considering the current perspective of existence?",
  "progress_halted_by_ai": "Everyone has an idea of what a qualia (totally inbuilt reaction) is, but not many are familiar with the experience machine thought experiment. The experience machine is nothing but a machine (or in this case a software or an AI) that simulates reality. According to the thought-experiment, you can choose only one of the following: a) live your life and experience the actual reality or b) use the experience machine and decide an amazing life-better reality. Further, in the experiment, many people would actually choose to use the experience machine and neglect the actual reality to save themselves from the harshness of the reality. If we were at the end of technological development and developed such an AI, what makes us believe that the AIs would not spend infinite time simulating a much easier world for the human race just to feed them with happiness, or some kind of positive qualia for that matter?"
}

